          "heading": "Extended unit perspective",
          "content": "A sedimentary section is a selective archive. Source rocks can weather for millions of years before their grains reach a depositional sink; after arrival, the same particles may be repeatedly eroded and redeposited. The geologist therefore distinguishes the time when a grain crystallized from the time when a bed accumulated. A fossil shell enclosed in mud, a detrital zircon in sandstone and a volcanic ash bed play different roles in dating. The rock also has more than one composition: framework grains, mud matrix, early marine cement and late burial cement may all record distinct sources of matter. An accurate log begins by separating these components. Students should practise sketching fabric, measuring bed thickness perpendicular to bedding, and documenting the uncertainty introduced by vegetation, talus or covered sections. A single grain-size curve can be useful, but a curve without stratigraphic position, sample mass and field context is a weak geological explanation.",
