          "heading": "Lesson 1.1 \u2014 The sedimentary source-to-sink system",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nWeathering liberates grains or solutes, transport sorts them, and accommodation permits preservation; sediment flux can change dramatically before the material reaches a basin. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nProvenance indicators, thickness patterns, modern sediment budgets and downstream grain-size trends each sample different portions of the system. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nA coarse deposit may record high transport capacity, but it could instead reflect erosion of a nearby coarse source with little downstream reworking. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nConstruct a sediment budget with input, temporary storage, bypass and output; compare adjacent reaches to find an unmeasured term. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Field notebook and facies recognition.** Write a two-column field record headed *observation* and *inference*. Enter grains, laminae, colour, contact shape and any measurable geometry in the observation column; reserve terms describing ancient rivers, shorelines or deep water for the inference column. Choose an outcrop reference scale suitable for the problem: centimetres for a ripple foreset, metres for a channel body, and kilometres for a basin map. Mark beds that cannot be observed because of cover. The same feature may be meaningful at one scale and misleading at another. Draw the simplest facies association consistent with the visible relationships and list one expected lateral equivalent. In your report do not call an exposure representative merely because it is accessible; accessibility itself may produce sampling bias.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 1: Net annual sediment export\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 560 thousand tonnes supplied, 110 in temporary channel storage and 70 on floodplain; no unmeasured input. The transparent classroom expression is `560-110-70`. Evaluating this expression produces **380 thousand tonnes per year**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nSediment is often stored and remobilized over periods much longer than an individual event, so present-day flux is not ancient accumulation rate. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nInterpret preservation as filtered transport history rather than as a continuous record of everything supplied.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **the sedimentary source-to-sink system** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 1 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
