          "heading": "Lesson 1.3 \u2014 Clastic textures and grain-size statistics",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nHydraulic sorting, abrasion, fragmentation and selective preservation influence the distribution of particle sizes and shapes. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nSieve curves, laser-diffraction distributions, petrographic counts and grain-shape measurements reveal related but nonidentical sediment properties. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nA well-sorted sample may have experienced sustained hydraulic selection, aeolian recycling or repeated shoreline reworking. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nCompare median, percentiles, skewness and counting uncertainty from replicate subsamples rather than relying on one visual adjective. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Hypothesis competition and counterexample.** Begin with a pair of incompatible explanations that both seem plausible from the first observation. Instead of collecting more features that merely illustrate the preferred story, identify the measurement on which the predictions disagree. Consider that one hypothesis may involve sediment transport while the other involves later erosion or cementation; these are not mutually exclusive unless the timing is explicitly constrained. Record the expected observation under each explanation before seeing any additional dataset. Evidence that fails to discriminate is still descriptive evidence, but it does not rank the competing models. Where a third process could imitate both, give that possibility a separate row in the reasoning table. The final verdict may be that present evidence supports only a family of environments or ages.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 3: Gravel fraction in sampled mass\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** sieve fractions of 340 g gravel, 920 g sand and 140 g mud; dry recovered mass. The transparent classroom expression is `100*340/(340+920+140)`. Evaluating this expression produces **24.2857 %**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nSampling and measurement techniques differ in shape assumptions, fines recovery and mass versus volume weighting. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nTreat texture as a partial process indicator requiring sedimentary structures and context.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **clastic textures and grain-size statistics** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 3 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
