          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nFramework grains, matrix, cement and heavy minerals reflect source composition plus weathering, transport and diagenetic filtering. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nDetrital zircon populations, QFL counts, rock fragments and resistant heavy mineral suites can illuminate sediment supply. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nSimilar quartz-rich sandstone can be generated by multiple source terrains after prolonged recycling and selective dissolution. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nCombine framework point counts with stratigraphic changes and one independent geochemical or geochronological constraint. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Comparative synthesis and communication.** Move from a single log to a lateral comparison. Propose which properties ought to remain similar between neighbouring sections and which may change as environments migrate. A marker bed is only a reliable time line when evidence establishes its correlation; similar sandstone colour is not such evidence. Choose a visual representation that distinguishes observed boundaries from extrapolated ones, perhaps using solid versus dashed lines and a brief uncertainty legend. Present at least one alternative explanation in the figure caption rather than burying disagreement in the final paragraph. A coherent story should preserve contradictions instead of quietly removing inconvenient beds. In the conclusion identify the smallest claim that would remain valid if your preferred process model were overturned.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 5: Quartz proportion of QFL framework\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 76 quartz points, 18 feldspar points and 6 lithic points; matrix and cement excluded. The transparent classroom expression is `100*76/(76+18+6)`. Evaluating this expression produces **76 %**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nHydraulic concentration and chemical instability change mineral abundance independently of source proportions. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nSeparate original source signal from the transport and burial filters that changed it.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **sediment composition and provenance** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 5 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
