          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.2 \u2014 Competence, capacity and Shields stress",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nEntrainment depends on hydrodynamic stress relative to submerged particle weight, with grain packing and cohesion modifying thresholds. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nBed grain distributions, critical motion in a flume and bedload measurements relate transport onset to material and boundary conditions. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nA large grain may be deposited by a brief high-stress flood, a debris flow, ice transport or subsequent erosion of finer surrounding material. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nCalculate an approximate Shields parameter and bracket the result under uncertain bed slope and hydraulic radius. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Measurement and dimensional reasoning.** For the numerical component, write the governing relation and then translate every quantity into a unit consistent with that relation. State whether thickness is true stratigraphic thickness or apparent width, whether grain size is a mass-weighted or number-weighted distribution, and whether a flux represents sediment delivered or rock preserved. A point estimate may hide a large physical uncertainty. Recalculate after changing one assumed input by ten percent, and distinguish the mathematical sensitivity from the real variability of that input. Present units in the plotted axis titles rather than only in figure captions. A sensible arithmetic result is not sufficient evidence that the equation is valid at a particular outcrop. Discuss whether equilibrium, uniform flow, conserved grain mass or constant deposition has been assumed.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 7: Approximate bed shear stress\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** rho=1000 kg/m3, hydraulic radius 0.8 m, energy slope 0.0016 and uniform-flow assumption. The transparent classroom expression is `1000*9.81*0.8*0.0016`. Evaluating this expression produces **12.5568 Pa**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nNatural cohesive beds, hiding/exposure effects and particle shape mean there is no single universal motion threshold. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nDistinguish the ability to move a particular particle from the total mass that a flow can carry.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **competence, capacity and shields stress** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 7 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
