          "heading": "Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Bedforms, dunes and migration",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nSediment transport and bed morphology interact as ripples or dunes migrate and create cross-stratification. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nBedform wavelength, crest orientation, stoss/lee geometry and time-lapse observations record migration direction and changing hydraulics. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nClimbing-ripple lamination records net aggradation and can occur under changing suspended-sediment supply, not simply high velocity. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nMeasure bedform displacement between observations and compute migration speed; compare with cross-set thickness in preserved strata. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Sampling design and data integrity.** Prepare an explicit sampling protocol: what interval is the population of interest, how will representative sites be selected, which fraction of the sediment is included, and what precision is possible? Replicate critical counts because sedimentary deposits are patchy in both space and time. A sieve sample, thin section and borehole log observe different volumes and may not sample equivalent properties. Record locations and orientation without inventing precision that was not measured. For destructive sampling, respect collection permits and institution rules. Store the original readings separately from any normalized percentages so that the transformation can be inspected later. Treat missing data as missing, not as zeros, and write down every exclusion criterion before producing an attractive final plot.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 9: Bedform migration distance\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** migration speed 0.14 m/day sustained 9 days; observed crest tracked reliably. The transparent classroom expression is `0.14*9`. Evaluating this expression produces **1.26 m**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nPreserved cross-set thickness is typically smaller than original bedform height, and erosional truncation produces selection bias. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nLink a measured sedimentary structure to a plausible bedform only after considering preservation potential.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **bedforms, dunes and migration** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 9 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
