          "content": "Depositional environments cannot be assigned from one diagnostic-looking structure in isolation. A channel sandstone may appear in a river, delta, estuary or submarine system; a mud drape can follow either tides or episodic river slack water. Facies analysis gains power from repeated associations and their lateral connections. Students should draw a three-dimensional conceptual block diagram and trace how a sand body thins, migrates or connects to neighbouring muddy facies. A vertical succession is a single path through a changing spatial landscape and can be modified by avulsion, erosion and changes in accommodation. This unit uses environment labels as hypotheses about process and geography, not immutable rock identities. Field tests focus on geometry, current directions, contact relations, biogenic structures and a plausible sediment-routing pathway. Statements about ancient climate require evidence beyond the general presence of sand or mud.",
