          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nLateral channel migration deposits point-bar successions; overbank flooding and soil formation create contrasting floodplain facies. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nLateral-accretion surfaces, fining-upward channel fills, levee silts, crevasse splays and palaeosols create a coupled record. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nA fining-upward log can form in a tidal channel or delta distributary as well as a meandering river. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nTrace accretion surfaces laterally and use floodplain indicators to distinguish alluvial from tidally influenced packages. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Measurement and dimensional reasoning.** For the numerical component, write the governing relation and then translate every quantity into a unit consistent with that relation. State whether thickness is true stratigraphic thickness or apparent width, whether grain size is a mass-weighted or number-weighted distribution, and whether a flux represents sediment delivered or rock preserved. A point estimate may hide a large physical uncertainty. Recalculate after changing one assumed input by ten percent, and distinguish the mathematical sensitivity from the real variability of that input. Present units in the plotted axis titles rather than only in figure captions. A sensible arithmetic result is not sufficient evidence that the equation is valid at a particular outcrop. Discuss whether equilibrium, uniform flow, conserved grain mass or constant deposition has been assumed.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 12: Channel-body width-to-depth ratio\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 84 m mapped preserved width and 3.5 m preserved maximum thickness; geometry simplified. The transparent classroom expression is `84/3.5`. Evaluating this expression produces **24 dimensionless**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nAvulsion can abandon a channel without the ideal lateral-migration sequence seen in textbooks. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nInterpret channel bodies in relation to floodplain preservation rather than treating every fining-upward cycle as identical.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **meandering rivers and floodplains** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 12 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
