          "heading": "Lesson 3.5 \u2014 Beaches, tidal flats and estuaries",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nWaves, tides and river discharge operate on overlapping time scales and preserve mixed sand/mud packages. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nHerringbone cross-strata, mud drapes, tidal bundles, wave ripples and bioturbation can indicate flow reversal and salinity gradients. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nBidirectional cross-sets may reflect shifting river channels rather than true tides, and individual mud drapes occur in nonmarine settings. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nMeasure repeated lamina thicknesses and compare bundles and ichnofacies with a shoreline-to-basin transect. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Comparative synthesis and communication.** Move from a single log to a lateral comparison. Propose which properties ought to remain similar between neighbouring sections and which may change as environments migrate. A marker bed is only a reliable time line when evidence establishes its correlation; similar sandstone colour is not such evidence. Choose a visual representation that distinguishes observed boundaries from extrapolated ones, perhaps using solid versus dashed lines and a brief uncertainty legend. Present at least one alternative explanation in the figure caption rather than burying disagreement in the final paragraph. A coherent story should preserve contradictions instead of quietly removing inconvenient beds. In the conclusion identify the smallest claim that would remain valid if your preferred process model were overturned.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 15: Average tidal bundle duration\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 28 hypothetical semidiurnal-tide bundles at half-day spacing; ideal uninterrupted preservation. The transparent classroom expression is `28*0.5`. Evaluating this expression produces **14 days**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nNeap\u2013spring cyclicity is often incompletely preserved and biological reworking blurs hydrodynamic signals. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nGive a tidal interpretation when several independent indicators agree, not solely a picturesque ripple pattern.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **beaches, tidal flats and estuaries** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 15 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
