          "content": "Marine sedimentation combines physical redistribution, biology, chemistry and burial alteration. Siliciclastic sand may be delivered to the shelf and remobilized by storms or gravity flows. Carbonate sediment may be produced near its final depositional location by organisms and chemical precipitation, then reworked and cemented. Neither carbonate production nor shelf-water depth is constant through time. Deep-water deposits pose another challenge: a thin bed might represent quiet background accumulation, the distal fraction of an event, or erosion followed by condensation. Core intervals are especially vulnerable to overinterpretation if lateral architecture cannot be seen. Students should construct a facies transition from proximal source to distal sink and keep depositional structures distinct from burial cement and fractures. Models and data demonstrate basic geometric or rate calculations, not realistic predictions of marine hazards or petroleum reservoirs.",
