          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nSediment gravity flows redistribute shelf and slope material into submarine channels, levees, lobes and basin floors. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nGraded beds, erosional channels, sole structures, thick-bedded lobe sandstones and muddy overbank levees can record flow pathways. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nDeep-water sand need not represent one classic turbidity current; debris flows and contour currents may contribute. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nRelate bed thickness and grain-size distributions across channel-axis, margin and distal lobe positions. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Sampling design and data integrity.** Prepare an explicit sampling protocol: what interval is the population of interest, how will representative sites be selected, which fraction of the sediment is included, and what precision is possible? Replicate critical counts because sedimentary deposits are patchy in both space and time. A sieve sample, thin section and borehole log observe different volumes and may not sample equivalent properties. Record locations and orientation without inventing precision that was not measured. For destructive sampling, respect collection permits and institution rules. Store the original readings separately from any normalized percentages so that the transformation can be inspected later. Treat missing data as missing, not as zeros, and write down every exclusion criterion before producing an attractive final plot.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 19: Submarine lobe grain-size fining\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 0.23 mm proximal median and 0.11 mm distal median in a synthetic turbidite lobe. The transparent classroom expression is `100*(0.23-0.11)/0.23`. Evaluating this expression produces **52.1739 % decline**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nSeafloor confinement, flow transformation and erosion modify ideal Bouma-type successions. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nUse spatial architecture plus bed-scale facies to distinguish channels, lobes and background suspension fallout.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **submarine canyons and turbidite systems** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 19 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
