          "heading": "Lesson 5.1 \u2014 Superposition, facing and cross-cutting relationships",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nIn an undeformed stratigraphic sequence, lower beds are generally older, but overturning, fault repetition and reworking require field tests. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nGraded bedding, load casts, cross-strata truncation, faults and intrusive contacts help establish facing and relative event order. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nAn apparent younging trend in a log may be inverted by tight folding or duplicated by a thrust. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nIntegrate multiple way-up structures with bedding-cleavage relations on a geological cross section. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Field notebook and facies recognition.** Write a two-column field record headed *observation* and *inference*. Enter grains, laminae, colour, contact shape and any measurable geometry in the observation column; reserve terms describing ancient rivers, shorelines or deep water for the inference column. Choose an outcrop reference scale suitable for the problem: centimetres for a ripple foreset, metres for a channel body, and kilometres for a basin map. Mark beds that cannot be observed because of cover. The same feature may be meaningful at one scale and misleading at another. Draw the simplest facies association consistent with the visible relationships and list one expected lateral equivalent. In your report do not call an exposure representative merely because it is accessible; accessibility itself may produce sampling bias.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 21: Fault-repeated section thickness\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 85 m apparent logged section and interpreted 20 m tectonically repeated interval. The transparent classroom expression is `85-20`. Evaluating this expression produces **65 m**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nEvery relative age inference assumes identified primary structures are correctly recognized and preserved. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nConstruct an event order, specifying what each contact proves and what it cannot prove.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **superposition, facing and cross-cutting relationships** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 21 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
