          "heading": "Lesson 5.2 \u2014 Formal lithostratigraphic units and type sections",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nA formation is primarily defined by recognizable lithological properties and stratigraphic relationships, not by a fixed span of geologic time. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nMappable lithologic character, lateral contacts and a designated reference section support consistent naming and correlation. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nLithologically similar sandstone units at different ages may be unrelated and should not be merged solely on rock colour. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nDefine observable criteria for a hypothetical formation and trace them across two measured columns. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Measurement and dimensional reasoning.** For the numerical component, write the governing relation and then translate every quantity into a unit consistent with that relation. State whether thickness is true stratigraphic thickness or apparent width, whether grain size is a mass-weighted or number-weighted distribution, and whether a flux represents sediment delivered or rock preserved. A point estimate may hide a large physical uncertainty. Recalculate after changing one assumed input by ten percent, and distinguish the mathematical sensitivity from the real variability of that input. Present units in the plotted axis titles rather than only in figure captions. A sensible arithmetic result is not sufficient evidence that the equation is valid at a particular outcrop. Discuss whether equilibrium, uniform flow, conserved grain mass or constant deposition has been assumed.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 22: Formation extent in map pixels\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** rectangular mapped outcrop footprint 3.2 km by 1.4 km; excludes covered portions. The transparent classroom expression is `3.2*1.4`. Evaluating this expression produces **4.48 km\u00b2**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nFacies changes cause boundaries to cross time lines, while poor exposure can make unit identity uncertain. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nDistinguish lithostratigraphic boundaries from chronostratigraphic boundaries explicitly.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **formal lithostratigraphic units and type sections** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 22 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
