          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nFirst and last observed fossil occurrences can define useful zones, but observed ranges depend on ecological and sampling limitations. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nAssemblages, index fossils and stratigraphic distribution charts permit relative placement of sections. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nA taxon may appear later in one basin because of migration or habitat change rather than later evolutionary origination. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nGraph fossil occurrences against height, compare multiple taxa and assess reworking before proposing correlation. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Hypothesis competition and counterexample.** Begin with a pair of incompatible explanations that both seem plausible from the first observation. Instead of collecting more features that merely illustrate the preferred story, identify the measurement on which the predictions disagree. Consider that one hypothesis may involve sediment transport while the other involves later erosion or cementation; these are not mutually exclusive unless the timing is explicitly constrained. Record the expected observation under each explanation before seeing any additional dataset. Evidence that fails to discriminate is still descriptive evidence, but it does not rank the competing models. Where a third process could imitate both, give that possibility a separate row in the reasoning table. The final verdict may be that present evidence supports only a family of environments or ages.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 23: Observed fossil overlap interval\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** taxon A observed from 48\u201374 m; taxon B from 54\u201367 m, heights relative to section base. The transparent classroom expression is `min(74,67)-max(48,54)`. Evaluating this expression produces **13 m**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nDiachronous migration, preservation and taxonomic revisions create important uncertainties in boundaries. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nState whether a fossil occurrence is a local datum, an inferred evolutionary event or a formal chronostratigraphic marker.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **biostratigraphy and fossil ranges** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 23 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
