          "secNumber": "5.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.5 \u2014 Radiometric dates and sedimentary time",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nDepositional ages may be constrained by interbedded volcanic ash or youngest detrital minerals, but individual mineral ages are not automatically bed ages. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nWell-characterized ash beds, detrital zircon spectra and multiple stratigraphic superpositions bound deposition intervals. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nThe youngest detrital zircon may precede deposition substantially if the sediment source did not provide contemporary zircon. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nPlace dated volcanic horizons on an age\u2013height plot and distinguish maximum depositional from crystallization ages. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Comparative synthesis and communication.** Move from a single log to a lateral comparison. Propose which properties ought to remain similar between neighbouring sections and which may change as environments migrate. A marker bed is only a reliable time line when evidence establishes its correlation; similar sandstone colour is not such evidence. Choose a visual representation that distinguishes observed boundaries from extrapolated ones, perhaps using solid versus dashed lines and a brief uncertainty legend. Present at least one alternative explanation in the figure caption rather than burying disagreement in the final paragraph. A coherent story should preserve contradictions instead of quietly removing inconvenient beds. In the conclusion identify the smallest claim that would remain valid if your preferred process model were overturned.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 25: Time between ash dates\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** older lower ash 102.4 Ma and younger upper ash 99.1 Ma; uncertainty omitted in point estimate. The transparent classroom expression is `102.4-99.1`. Evaluating this expression produces **3.3 Myr**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nReworking, analytical discordance and unrecognized hiatuses can invalidate a na\u00efve linear age model. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nUse dated horizons as constraints with uncertainties and account for missing time.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **radiometric dates and sedimentary time** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 25 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
