          "secNumber": "6.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 6.5 \u2014 Basin subsidence, compaction and backstripping",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nBurial compacts porous sediment; reconstructing older basin geometry requires assumptions about porosity-depth trends and palaeowater depth. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nWell logs, decompacted thicknesses, stratigraphic ages and fault maps support long-term basin-subsidence analysis. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nApparent acceleration in accumulation can reflect a changing sediment supply or preservation bias instead of tectonic subsidence. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nApply a simple porosity correction to a layer, then compare alternative initial porosities and compaction lengths. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Comparative synthesis and communication.** Move from a single log to a lateral comparison. Propose which properties ought to remain similar between neighbouring sections and which may change as environments migrate. A marker bed is only a reliable time line when evidence establishes its correlation; similar sandstone colour is not such evidence. Choose a visual representation that distinguishes observed boundaries from extrapolated ones, perhaps using solid versus dashed lines and a brief uncertainty legend. Present at least one alternative explanation in the figure caption rather than burying disagreement in the final paragraph. A coherent story should preserve contradictions instead of quietly removing inconvenient beds. In the conclusion identify the smallest claim that would remain valid if your preferred process model were overturned.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 30: Decompacted sediment thickness\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 12 m present bed with porosity 0.22; inferred initial porosity 0.44; solid volume conserved. The transparent classroom expression is `12*(1-0.22)/(1-0.44)`. Evaluating this expression produces **16.7143 m**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nErosion removes evidence; compaction parameters and palaeobathymetry are uncertain. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nPresent subsidence curves as model-dependent reconstructions, not direct measurements.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **basin subsidence, compaction and backstripping** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 30 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
