          "secNumber": "7.2",
          "heading": "Extended unit perspective",
          "content": "Sedimentary rocks commonly acquire a second and third history while buried. Uncemented sand compacts, develops contacts, dissolves unstable grains and receives pore-filling minerals. Shallow depositional porosity is therefore different from present pore geometry. Chemical measurements also require caution: an isotope ratio recorded in a limestone can be influenced by the water and reaction conditions of later diagenesis. Biological mixing may destroy lamination while preserving evidence of benthic life. Students should prepare an event sequence using cross-cutting relations among grains, cements, pores and fractures. They should contrast a measured value with the proxy interpretation derived from it. A synthetic geochemical trend is useful for practising uncertainty propagation but is not a calibrated palaeoclimate reconstruction. Lab and field safety rules remain applicable for acid testing, mineral powders and core-handling equipment.",
