          "secNumber": "7.3",
          "heading": "Lesson 7.1 \u2014 Compaction and porosity loss",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nMechanical grain rearrangement and chemical pressure solution decrease pore volume as overburden and burial temperature increase. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nGrain contacts, stylolites, deformed ductile grains and porosity gradients show multiple compaction pathways. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nLow porosity might reflect early cementation rather than deep burial and intense compaction. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nCalculate bulk porosity from grain and bulk densities; compare thin-section point counts with plug-scale measurements. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Field notebook and facies recognition.** Write a two-column field record headed *observation* and *inference*. Enter grains, laminae, colour, contact shape and any measurable geometry in the observation column; reserve terms describing ancient rivers, shorelines or deep water for the inference column. Choose an outcrop reference scale suitable for the problem: centimetres for a ripple foreset, metres for a channel body, and kilometres for a basin map. Mark beds that cannot be observed because of cover. The same feature may be meaningful at one scale and misleading at another. Draw the simplest facies association consistent with the visible relationships and list one expected lateral equivalent. In your report do not call an exposure representative merely because it is accessible; accessibility itself may produce sampling bias.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 31: Bulk porosity from density\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** dry bulk density 2150 kg/m3 and grain density 2650 kg/m3, no cracks or fluid mass. The transparent classroom expression is `100*(1-2150/2650)`. Evaluating this expression produces **18.8679 %**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nMicroporosity and fracture pores vary with scale; one core plug may not represent an entire interval. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nIdentify which part of the porosity history was depositional and which was diagenetic.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **compaction and porosity loss** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 31 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
