          "secNumber": "7.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 7.2 \u2014 Cementation and dissolution histories",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nCarbonate, silica and clay cements alter sediment rigidity and permeability; dissolution may create secondary pores. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nCathodoluminescence zoning, cement overgrowths, cross-cutting pores and grain-coating minerals can distinguish episodes. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nHigh quartz-cement volume can reflect long burial, silica supply or favourable nucleation rather than only maximum temperature. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nBuild a paragenetic sequence from pore-filling and cross-cutting relations and check regional fluid-flow history. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Measurement and dimensional reasoning.** For the numerical component, write the governing relation and then translate every quantity into a unit consistent with that relation. State whether thickness is true stratigraphic thickness or apparent width, whether grain size is a mass-weighted or number-weighted distribution, and whether a flux represents sediment delivered or rock preserved. A point estimate may hide a large physical uncertainty. Recalculate after changing one assumed input by ten percent, and distinguish the mathematical sensitivity from the real variability of that input. Present units in the plotted axis titles rather than only in figure captions. A sensible arithmetic result is not sufficient evidence that the equation is valid at a particular outcrop. Discuss whether equilibrium, uniform flow, conserved grain mass or constant deposition has been assumed.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 32: Cement volume by point count\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 81 cement points in 900 random petrographic point observations; classification errors ignored. The transparent classroom expression is `100*81/900`. Evaluating this expression produces **9 %**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nReaction rates depend on reactive surfaces, pH, salinity and flow pathways that may not be preserved. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nEstablish the order of events before inferring fluid composition from one cement generation.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **cementation and dissolution histories** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 32 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
