          "heading": "Lesson 7.5 \u2014 Ichnology and bioturbation",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nBurrowing organisms modify sediments and preserve information about oxygenation, substrate, energy and ecology. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nTrace-fossil assemblages, burrow cross-cutting and the degree of sediment mixing provide contextual environmental evidence. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nLow trace-fossil diversity could reflect salinity stress, rapid deposition or poor preservation, not only oxygen depletion. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nRecord ichnofabric intensity across facies and compare independent oxygen or salinity indicators. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Comparative synthesis and communication.** Move from a single log to a lateral comparison. Propose which properties ought to remain similar between neighbouring sections and which may change as environments migrate. A marker bed is only a reliable time line when evidence establishes its correlation; similar sandstone colour is not such evidence. Choose a visual representation that distinguishes observed boundaries from extrapolated ones, perhaps using solid versus dashed lines and a brief uncertainty legend. Present at least one alternative explanation in the figure caption rather than burying disagreement in the final paragraph. A coherent story should preserve contradictions instead of quietly removing inconvenient beds. In the conclusion identify the smallest claim that would remain valid if your preferred process model were overturned.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 35: Bioturbated fraction\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** 54 cm visually mottled within 120 cm section; length ratio not volume fraction. The transparent classroom expression is `100*54/120`. Evaluating this expression produces **45 %**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nTime averaging, compaction and recognition bias influence trace-fossil patterns. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nInterpret ecological constraints from assemblages and host facies, not from a single burrow shape.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **ichnology and bioturbation** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 35 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata.",
