          "secNumber": "8.2",
          "heading": "Extended unit perspective",
          "content": "Integration requires more discipline than naming every plausible process. A basin reconstruction is a model of deposition, erosion, time and preserved geometry supported by incomplete observations. A good interpretation includes predictions about places that were not used to fit the original conceptual diagram. Hold back one section, test the reconstructed facies belt there and record failure honestly. Correlations should identify whether the line follows a rock unit, fossil datum, age marker or interpreted flooding surface. These lines need not coincide. The final report should include a sediment budget, a correlation panel, one quantified uncertainty and a ranked list of field observations most likely to settle the remaining disagreement. Applications to engineering, flood risk or subsurface resource decisions require separate site-specific professional methods. The purpose of a textbook model is to sharpen questions and identify evidence, not to license high-stakes operational decisions.",
