          "heading": "Lesson 8.5 \u2014 Capstone: conflicting evidence in a changing basin",
          "content": "### What is being explained?\n\nSynthetic stratigraphic records can contain erosional gaps, diachronous shoreline facies and diagenetic overprints that resist a unique history. Explain which visible feature would distinguish this process from later sedimentary reworking or burial modification.\n\n### The observational record\n\nA combined facies panel, two ash ages, a fossil datum and ambiguous isotope shift create multiple defensible interpretations. Record the scale and instrument behind each observation; do not convert a field description into a claim about ancient environment without additional evidence.\n\n### Competing interpretations\n\nOne model may prioritize relative sea level while another emphasizes shifting sediment supply and basin subsidence. Describe at least one predicted feature that differs between the rival and leading model, and identify a realistic observation that would distinguish them.\n\n### A discriminating investigation\n\nMake a ranked list of proposed tests, include a sensitivity analysis and write a short manuscript with an uncertainty map. Specify the comparison material and observable decision rule so that a negative test outcome could change your preferred interpretation.\n\n**Comparative synthesis and communication.** Move from a single log to a lateral comparison. Propose which properties ought to remain similar between neighbouring sections and which may change as environments migrate. A marker bed is only a reliable time line when evidence establishes its correlation; similar sandstone colour is not such evidence. Choose a visual representation that distinguishes observed boundaries from extrapolated ones, perhaps using solid versus dashed lines and a brief uncertainty legend. Present at least one alternative explanation in the figure caption rather than burying disagreement in the final paragraph. A coherent story should preserve contradictions instead of quietly removing inconvenient beds. In the conclusion identify the smallest claim that would remain valid if your preferred process model were overturned.\n\n### Worked quantitative exercise 40: Synthetic capstone average accumulation\n\n**Hypothetical inputs:** aggregate 73 m from three preserved units over interpreted 1.5 Myr including unmeasured gaps. The transparent classroom expression is `(23+31+19)/(1.5)`. Evaluating this expression produces **48.6667 m/Myr**. Identify the numerator and denominator when the calculation produces a percentage, and write each conversion between metres, kilometres, days, years or millions of years explicitly. If the result is a transport rate, do not describe it as an observed sedimentation rate unless storage and bypass are accounted for; if it is a thickness, identify whether erosion and compaction are represented.\n\n### Evidence limitations and a qualified conclusion\n\nAmbiguity may remain even after every available dataset is used; a model need not deliver a unique history. Categorize the dominant uncertainty as sampling, analytical, chronological, mechanistic or preservation-related, and name the measurement that would reduce it.\n\nDefend the smallest set of conclusions that all credible models share and state which additional observation would decide among them.\n\n### Practice and evidence checks\n\n1. Describe **capstone: conflicting evidence in a changing basin** using three observations that do not yet assume a depositional setting.\n2. State the physical reason the proposed mechanism would produce one of those observations, then identify a contrary observation that would count against it.\n3. Recalculate Exercise 40 after modifying one stated parameter, document units and explain sensitivity.\n4. Design an independent test using the protocol above; state a clear predicted outcome for the leading model and for its rival.\n5. Write an 80-word report that explains what is known, what remains uncertain and why the distinction matters when interpreting preserved strata."